WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.180
Lee Trevino's Fighting Golf!

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:07.940
Never before in a home format have so many options been available to a player of video golf.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.580
And welcome to NEStalgia!

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.000
Chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.900
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:34.680
And I'm Joe.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:40.240
And guys, that quote that we read at the beginning of the episode is brought to you by the U.S.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.200
National Video Game Team of 1988.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.280
Is that like an Olympic team?

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.260
Yeah, that's what it sounds like.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:51.180
I had a feeling that nobody would know who the U.S.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.520
National Video Game Team is, so I did some research because I had never heard of them either.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.500
And it was set up by Walter Day back in 1983.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:01.780
Do you guys know Walter Day?

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.960
He's that guy who wears the referee shirt in King of Kong?

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:09.840
I mean, I must have seen him, but I don't recall.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.900
He's like the owner of Twin Galaxies 2 or something of that effect.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:19.840
And Twin Galaxies are the people that used to hold all the world records for the arcade games.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:32.820
Anyway, to make a long story short, Walter Day set up a team that involved like Billy Mitchell and Todd Rogers, two people that are now like banned from video game achievements for life because they both got caught cheating.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.780
And they toured the U.S.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:48.440
in like a bus going around beating people at different video game arcades throughout America and then eventually went all the way to going to foreign embassies and beating international video game players as well.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.060
Okay, I like that.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:58.060
Like when you just say beating, it sounds like they went in a tour bus just beating crowds of people in arcades.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:07.140
But now when you say embassies, now that I'm confused because like what foreign embassy would allow such a thing to occur?

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.720
Did they actually care about video games back then on the international stage?

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:20.160
And was this like, I mean, was this just like a thing that they did or were there other like teams that they were fighting against that were also trying to do this?

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:24.960
Or were they just like challengers, let us know and we'll find you and fight you.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:34.000
Yeah. So were they just doing this on their own or were there other teams that they could go out and were they just like any challengers, let us know and we'll come fight you or were there rival factions?

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.700
I don't think there were any rival factions.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.620
But that's something I guess I should look up.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.960
You know, there's not a lot of information on how they did any of this.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:55.420
It does seem like they took it upon themselves to name themselves the US national video game team and then just showed up at random arcades around the United States and said, bet I could beat you in Pac-Man.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:58.160
And they played a guy who had memorized Pac-Man.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:05.840
So it's just a bunch of kids like making a club and and just doing doing things.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.660
Kids would be the wrong word.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:11.220
You know, like 20s.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.360
Is this a legal organization?

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:15.540
Apparently so.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:19.240
Apparently, yes. And they're even to this day, they're still active.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:24.500
You can look up their current members on their website, US national video game team dot com.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:27.420
Okay. It's actually a Zanga.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:29.440
But anyway, continue, Mike.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.100
Yeah. Anyway, remember golf on the NES?

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.440
Oh, you mean one of your favorite games of all time, golf?

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.760
We all loved golf.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.000
Joe, didn't you love golf?

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.200
I loved golf.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:44.600
I did love golf. Not as much as you, granted, but I did like golf.

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:51.600
Golf. And now we have Lee Trevino's fighting golf, which is not a beat him up.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:55.000
And it's also not a it's not a fighter either.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:56.220
It is just golf.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.960
And then Lee Trevino is not in it.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:00.320
Well, he's in it.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.260
He's in it. Make no mistake about it.

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:04.220
He's in it. Yeah.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:05.260
He's super mechs.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:16.780
See, it's funny because I didn't know when I when I saw this game on the list, I had to do some digging just to understand why it's named the way it is.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:25.260
And like I learned that Lee Trevino was, you know, he was the equivalent of like a sort of like a Tiger Woods of the time.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:38.120
And that can't just that doesn't stop me, though, from sort of fantasizing about this game as if it were something along the lines of like Sean Avery's 3D hockey.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:45.160
Like it just seems so like a pedestrian name to be attached to a game series.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:48.340
I don't know if this has another one, but I don't know what you guys thought.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:51.940
Well, did you did you find a reason that it's fighting golf?

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:54.780
I don't know why it's called fighting golf.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:57.480
I thought maybe Lee Trevino had a temper.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:04:59.900
But that's just a guess.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:02.100
Yeah, because I was waiting for that.

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:03.220
I was like, oh, it's gonna be cool.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.880
There's gonna be like a little twist where you where you have to go like on a mission and fight people or something.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:08.820
But no, it's golf.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:12.480
Well, there is actually an explanation for for both things.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:13.840
I'll talk about both points.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:21.420
So first, for the fighting golf, that's actually just strictly a mistranslation by the localization team,

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:28.980
where they just didn't understand the title of the Japanese game and included fighting thinking that like, oh, yeah, that's an actiony word.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:35.820
That'll make sense. I yeah, I kind of figured it was like maybe it was supposed to be like versus or something because there's two player in it or something.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:37.460
It was mistranslated to fighting.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:38.600
But I didn't know. Yeah.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.800
And then Sean, just to just to go back to your thing, because I think Sean Avery's 3D hockey is very funny.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:54.140
It's true that like that would even be a thing that would go on to happen, at least in baseball games where like Nintendo got like Ken Griffey's license.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:56.460
And that was like incredible for the time.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:58.440
And so yeah, that's Ken Griffey though.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:05:59.800
Right. No, but that's what I'm saying.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:04.240
Like anybody else who wanted to make a baseball game had to use a different player.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:12.680
And so that's kind of like a real life example of like, why would I play Mike Piazza's Strike Zone when Ken Griffey Jr.'s Slugout is happening?

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:15.400
So wait, Mike Piazza's Strike Zone is a game.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.200
It's a game on the N64 and it's awful.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:37.100
Okay. Also, since we're talking about Lee Trevino's fighting golf and Lee Trevino in general here, why would Lee Trevino opt himself to be in a game where he's the second most powerful golfer and has the second worst control out of all of the golfers?

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:38.640
I think it's...

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:42.500
I feel like he probably had no idea what they were going to do to his likeness.

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:49.180
I think that maybe he just wants to be seen as the all-rounder.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:52.120
And, you know, because the people that he's...

00:06:52.120 --> 00:07:00.380
The other characters in this game, if we want to go there, is a girl that is just defined by how pretty she is.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:02.760
And she's more accurate.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:08.300
And there's another dude that is just strong and maybe he never even played golf before.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:09.740
Oh, you're talking about Big Jumbo?

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.180
Big Jumbo.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.440
And I forgot who the other one is.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.280
But I don't remember what...

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:17.420
Miracle Chasuke.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.520
Yeah. So, I don't know.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:24.440
Maybe he just wanted to be seen as, like, the only true golfer on the roster.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:33.080
Right. And Lee Trevino is Supermex, the third option on the character screen, because that was his nickname, was Supermex.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:34.960
And I think that...

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:41.460
Like, I'm sure there's a perfectly valid explanation for that, but the only reason I could find is that he was a Mexican-American golf player.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:42.960
So... Apparently, yeah.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:44.060
They just naturally called him that.

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:45.820
I guess that is true.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:51.760
Like, even on the infallible Wikipedia page, they said that he was affectionately called Supermex.

00:07:53.140 --> 00:08:00.640
But it's important to mention that there are characters here, because I think that's, like, a big differentiator right off the bat from, like, NES golf.

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:07.820
In the case that, like, each of these characters, even if they are no-name people outside of Lee Trevino, they each control differently.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:11.800
You have pretty Amy, who has the least amount of power, but the best control.

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:17.380
And, yeah, it's weird that, like, her whole personality is, like, defined by her being pretty.

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:19.180
But what's up with, like, the...

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:24.020
What's up with the fact that if she hits, like, a great shot, you also get, like...

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:25.320
You get to see her panties.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:27.400
Yeah, it's weird. Like, what's that about?

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:28.740
It's very Japanese.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:37.580
Nice. And then you have Big Jumbo, who, as you can imagine, has the most power, but sucks at everything else.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:40.020
So you have to, like, work with that against you.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:43.480
Supermex, like I said, he's just this...

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:48.720
He's just an all-around average golfer, which just seems strange for Lee Trevino to want to attach his name to.

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:50.160
No, it makes...

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:53.380
It sounds better if you just say he's an all-around golfer.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:54.720
Oh, there you go.

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:56.460
Got it. All-around golfer.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:18.540
Yeah. And Miracle Chasuke, who I'm sure I'm not saying that last name right, but his whole thing is that he can do the Miracle putt, which is, like, he has a better opportunity of, like, if you're using a, like, a sand wedge or a power wedge and you're not on the green, you have a better opportunity of hitting it into the hole than anybody else.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:22.040
So you're saying there's, like, RNG in this game?

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:28.020
I'm saying that there's a lot of RNG just off the bat based on those introductions.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:35.460
I mean, now you have to choose between, like, forget about understanding the controls of a golf game in two dimensions, right?

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:42.020
Like, forget about those things for a second and understand that now, no matter who you pick, you're penalized in some way.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:56.700
Maybe that's why it makes sense that Lee Trevino is the all-rounder because that's what, like, most people who are going to play are just going to probably feel more comfortable playing with him because it's like, well, he's pretty good at everything, even though he's not the best.

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:06.240
And I also just disagree with the notion that these are, like, that your experience is lesser because there is, like, a variety of kinds of characters.

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:08.120
I mean, I think it's just in how you frame it.

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:21.560
Like, yeah, I mean, I think unless it's just going to be a completely cosmetic difference between what character you choose, like, there should be pros and cons to choosing them.

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:22.560
Yeah, I agree with that.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:25.180
I didn't, it didn't bother me that they had different attributes.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:28.000
Granted, I really mostly just played Supermax.

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:33.940
Yeah, I just feel like golf is already kind of complicated on the NES.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:36.080
Like, there's a lot working against it.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:44.020
And the idea that, like, there's randomness to your player character only makes it harder to actually be any good at this game.

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:55.980
Like, the only randomness that I really, that I can see in just the descriptions of these characters, though, is apparently that, that Miracle Chice K guy could get Miracle Putts.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:02.000
Everything else is just, you see it reflected on their UI.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:09.640
Like, if you're, if you're playing as Lee Trevino, like, everything behaves in one way.

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:16.400
But if you're playing as Amy, that little power slash accuracy gauge goes, goes a little slower.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:21.740
So, I, they're more like the tools afforded to you behave differently.

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:24.480
And I don't really see the RNG in it.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:31.780
So, I'm not, I guess I'm not talking about RNG in the sense of, like, that the computer actually programs randomness.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:38.860
What I'm saying is, is that there are accurate, there's measures of how hard each player can hit a ball.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.240
And so, obviously, like, Big Jumbo could hit something 275 yards where Pretty Amy can only hit a 225.

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:47.260
But Pretty Amy has better control over the ball.

00:11:47.260 --> 00:12:01.360
And that matters about, like, how the ball lands, you know, versus, like, if you're Big Jumbo, even if you hit it right on the middle, there's going to be some kind of slice for the ball that's going to cause it to wane left or right on any given particular putt.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:02.400
Hmm. Okay.

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:05.760
I didn't play as him, so maybe, maybe that is the case.

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:12.900
But I thought it was just that there was a, it was more sensitive to your input, not so much that it would add randomness to your input.

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:18.400
Right. No, it adds that, but on top of that, too, it's like, you know, each thing has their own thing, too.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:24.680
Like, Miracle Chesuke, his putting is great, but it comes at the cost of control and power.

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:32.040
And Pretty Amy is, you know, she has the least amount of power, but her putting isn't as good as Miracle Chesuke.

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:35.820
So it's like, it's just, it's interesting that they gave these characters a thing.

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:47.540
I guess I'm arguing that on an NES golf game, do we really need another layer of complications on top of the things that we've already experienced and the new additions that this game brings to it?

00:12:48.440 --> 00:13:04.680
I see what you're saying, but I do think that, I do, yeah, I do think that after, after playing it for a little bit, like, I think it's overall a good thing, at least in my opinion, also to have, granted, if there's anything that's like actually like punishing about picking a character, that might not be the best.

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:11.020
But I think the fact that it's like, just some characters have better attributes and better skills in one attribute than another character.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:14.620
I like that, too, because it adds a little bit of level of like strategy and choice to it.

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:04.620
Cool.

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:33.040
Yeah, I think that's a little bit more helpful than just always having to pick your own wedge or iron.

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:38.320
Yeah, I actually very rarely ended up finding myself switching it once in a while I did.

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:41.500
But for the most part, I just kind of trusted what the computer gave me.

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:45.200
And usually, I mean, obviously, I don't know what would have happened if I switched it.

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.820
But usually it felt like the computer gave me the right choice.

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:50.280
Yeah, which I don't know if that's a good thing or a bad thing.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:53.460
I liked it because I'm not super familiar with golf clubs.

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:59.940
And, you know, I didn't have to like think about it or like remember which was which or, you know, which one would be good and which in which position.

00:14:59.940 --> 00:15:04.180
But I'm sure after a while it might get like a little bit too easy.

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:06.360
I think this is the natural progression.

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:15.800
Like I wouldn't say that they were trailblazers for doing this, but there's a reason that golf games to this day will will sort of do that for you.

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:23.940
And part of it is because a layperson doesn't know the difference between a nine iron and a five wood like I don't.

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:39.020
So when I was just sort of trying like blindly looking at all of my clubs and deciding what to do in the originally as golf, like I just sort of had to like just guess what these things meant.

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:46.140
And I appreciate that at least like guides you to where you should be and then you can adjust it from there.

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:55.980
Like there were some instances where I went from a nine iron to the actual like driver and just to just to get some extra oomph to it.

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:59.400
But I think that this was the next logical step.

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:07.400
Yeah, I think a nice compromise for me would have been if there was like that like an easy mode that selects your clubs for you, but still displays them.

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:12.780
So you play that for a while, kind of learn the clubs and then like a regular or hard mode where it's like now.

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:24.880
Yeah. And it's not always the best, too, which is important, too, because I think it would stink if like it always chose the optimal thing for you, given a situation like it doesn't account for the wind or it doesn't account for like.

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:31.500
Like, oh, there might be a lake hazard in the middle of this and now you might land in there instead if you don't change your club.

00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:37.960
Like I like that because if it just chose you the optimal thing based on computer metrics, you'd be barely playing the game.

00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:41.160
It would just always swing as hard as possible and aim for the middle.

00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:44.580
I also think exactly.

00:16:44.860 --> 00:17:03.260
Yeah. Yeah, I also think that the ability to like to keep the clubs within the same amount that the NES golf game had wasn't a like hindrance for it because I'm pretty sure that's just the standard amount of clubs that anyone brings with them to a an 18 hole course.

00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:06.080
So that wasn't like that wasn't weird to me.

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:10.460
And at the same time, like I don't even know if I needed all of those options.

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:18.240
Like it's really nice for them to include everything from a one wood to a sand wedge, but I don't even know if I need all three iron to nine iron for me.

00:17:18.520 --> 00:17:30.340
I think it's just I guess it would be sort of like a I don't even know what what an equivalent would be, but it's like in a baseball game, you could only do a fastball or something.

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:37.240
I don't know. I think that exactly the different kinds of clubs are are very descriptive of the sport.

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:39.000
So you need them, I think.

00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:46.760
Yeah, although it is. I do wonder like how much like those small increments actually do within like the hardware of the NES.

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:50.880
Like maybe they don't make too much of a difference, but they're just there for immersion.

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:56.420
I'm not sure, but it just seems like how much can the NES be really differentiating?

00:17:56.580 --> 00:18:00.000
I think it's just I think it's just differences in in range.

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:06.200
And maybe how sensitive it is to changes in power.

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:07.660
I don't know. That's just a guess.

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:14.620
No, I think I think you're spot on there because it seems like for the most part, each club is and is regardless of player,

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:26.280
because even if your max is 275 or your max is 245, it seems to go down in increments of like 30s and then 10s and then maybe even as low as 5s towards the end.

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:34.860
So it seems like it was nice of them to like not say like, oh, well, you really only need a one wood, a four wood, a one iron, a five iron and a nine iron.

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:36.700
You know, like it's nice to include everything.

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:41.840
And then the wedges are there for the special instances in which you've gotten yourself into trouble.

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:51.280
Yeah. And there's more movement on the ball than ever, too, because you can now use your D pad to do like high and low hits and backspins.

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:52.840
Did you guys try any of that stuff?

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:58.460
I tried backspin a little bit, but it was tough because it's like it seems like it's a very precise timing.

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:00.860
I didn't really try too much else, though.

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:04.860
I wasn't very good at it, so I don't think I ever actually pulled it off.

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:17.700
But I appreciate that it at least has a little bit more realism in this game than in modern golf games where you have this magical ability to alter the course of your ball in mid flight.

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:19.980
And like that's where you add your spin as well.

00:19:20.200 --> 00:19:23.380
The ball is in the air, which doesn't really make sense to me.

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:32.880
I don't know if you guys have played more recent golf games, but it at least was was trying to simulate that aspect of the sport.

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:38.100
The only person who was ever able to control their golf ball in midair was Michael Jordan in Space Jam.

00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:47.920
Exactly. I will echo what Joe was saying there that, you know, it might just be too many movements to do a backspin.

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:56.400
It's just a little complicated because you have to time it appropriately after your movement with the rest of the power gauges.

00:19:56.540 --> 00:19:59.360
So you have to like perform everything that you need to do.

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:04.800
And then also think about like using the right or left D pad to put a little swerve on it right before you tee off.

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:08.260
But you haven't, you know, you haven't, you've already hit A twice.

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:10.640
So it's like A, A, left, A.

00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:16.200
And I just think that's like, for me, that's too much to think about in golf right now on this platform.

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:18.300
It's actually, that's where the fighting comes from.

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:19.120
Yeah, it's a lot to think about within like one second.

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:24.520
That's where the fighting comes from because it actually has the, yeah, they have to do combos to get the ball anywhere.

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:30.500
But high and low work really well because those are just holding the upper down on your D pad,

00:20:30.620 --> 00:20:33.100
depending on if you want to hit the ball high or if you want to hit it low.

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:40.180
And it only has one effect, which is just that if you hit the ball high, the wind accounts for more effect.

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:44.360
So if you want the wind to like, you know, screw up your ball a little more,

00:20:44.540 --> 00:20:51.220
if you, if it's going like 10, 10 miles per hour north and you want to make sure you carry your ball, you want to hit high.

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:56.960
Whereas like, if you don't want to be affected by the wind, but don't care about shooting like very far,

00:20:57.140 --> 00:21:00.880
then you want to shoot low to make sure that you get the most accurate possible hit.

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:04.820
So, I mean, those are great inclusions that were definitely not on NES golf.

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:06.100
So that's cool. Indubitably.

00:21:06.260 --> 00:21:12.320
And while we're talking about the D pad, the left and right don't do anything while you're shooting, correct?

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:17.520
I don't know. It's only, I think that I'm pretty sure it's just up and down while you're shooting.

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:25.860
Is that how you apply, is that how you apply like hook or is that just, you do hook via where you do that second A press?

00:21:26.260 --> 00:21:27.220
Where you do that second A press.

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:28.740
I thought hook was where that second A press, yeah.

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:40.140
Yeah. So I was, I know this is like a, this is like such a tiny pet peeve, but I was like a little annoyed that I had to, every time I wanted to, you know, rotate my player at all.

00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:47.240
You have to press B to like activate the ability to rotate, even though the buttons you use to rotate are not doing anything beforehand.

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:48.420
Like there was no need for that.

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:53.920
So you have to press B to activate that ability, then press B to select club and then press B again to go back to swinging.

00:21:54.620 --> 00:21:57.540
And I know that's like a tiny thing, but it got annoying to me after a while.

00:21:57.680 --> 00:21:59.980
I kept forgetting like, wait, am I, am I swinging now?

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:03.540
Am I selecting my club? I feel like it should have just been B to select your club.

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:09.520
And that's the only, you don't need to have like an alternative control scheme just so you could turn left or right.

00:22:09.660 --> 00:22:11.960
If you have these two buttons that are not doing anything otherwise.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:14.480
Sure. I guess I agree with that.

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:22.580
I didn't bother me as much, but if, if it can be, uh, if it can be achieved and simplified, then why not?

00:22:23.160 --> 00:22:27.780
Yeah. I feel like maybe it, or, or then if you're going to have that thing, which wouldn't be that big of a deal.

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:32.260
If, if left and right, we're doing anything in the first control screen scheme.

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:35.960
I hope I'm, I hope I'm saying this in a clear enough way that the listeners know what I'm talking about.

00:22:36.220 --> 00:22:42.260
Uh, if you haven't played it, but I guess, but it's just, I guess we could, it's just a little weird that you have to go to like another page of controls.

00:22:42.560 --> 00:22:45.760
I guess like we could just very briefly like explain.

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:50.060
So there, there are like, there are three modes that you're going to cycle through.

00:22:50.060 --> 00:22:56.560
Uh, the first is, uh, you have a little cursor that shows the direction you're facing.

00:22:57.080 --> 00:22:59.740
Um, and you can adjust that with left and right.

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:04.680
And also sort of extrapolate distance from that point by pressing up and down.

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:11.620
And then if you press a or press B again, it'll, uh, move you to your club selection.

00:23:11.820 --> 00:23:15.600
If you press B again, it gets you to where you can actually take your swing.

00:23:15.780 --> 00:23:17.340
And that's just the power gauge.

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:25.660
And the second press of that power gauge determines, uh, how much hook or push, I guess you have on the swing.

00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:34.500
So what, what, what Joe is trying to describe is, uh, like there's a little too much division in how all those things control.

00:23:34.780 --> 00:23:42.400
Does that same power gauge, uh, selection could also handle turning left or right.

00:23:42.460 --> 00:23:43.380
Is that pretty clear?

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:48.740
Yeah, exactly. Like it's like they, they could be in the same screen.

00:23:48.740 --> 00:23:58.000
You know, you shouldn't have to switch to a new control scheme because that just, it just adds like a weird layer of complication that kind of sort of immersion breaking a little bit.

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:05.420
Uh, I know that I'm like overly annoyed with this like tiny little thing, but I just felt like after a while, I was just like, why is it making me do this every time?

00:24:05.560 --> 00:24:09.740
Well, it's important to note too, that you start on the assumption that you're swinging.

00:24:09.740 --> 00:24:21.840
So every time that you do want to move, uh, even just the tiniest bit rotate in a particular direction, you need to press the B button at least three times to get through your entire rotation.

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:28.580
You know? So, uh, it's, it'd be one thing if it asks you first, where do you want to face before that?

00:24:28.780 --> 00:24:31.000
You know, cause that would be like one way to achieve it.

00:24:31.100 --> 00:24:37.920
So you only have to do one B press, but you're going to wind up doing the B button press three times before you're able to go back to your swinging.

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:40.760
Right. And before I, I mean, I didn't read the manual right off the bat.

00:24:40.900 --> 00:24:50.100
I had to go back into it, but like at first I wasn't even sure how to turn because the, the D pad wasn't doing anything to make me turn, which then I just felt like, why doesn't it just let me turn now?

00:24:50.900 --> 00:24:54.920
But, uh, so I had to go in and read the manual and realize that you can like switch to another control.

00:24:55.240 --> 00:24:59.900
Yeah. The only thing I can think of is that, you know, you were asking if the left and right D pad do anything.

00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:15.540
Um, and it, it only happens when you're in the middle of, uh, you know, applying your swing where you can apply the, the, the backspin, but you know, that doesn't mean that you couldn't use the left and right beforehand, but maybe it was locking it off just this way.

00:25:15.680 --> 00:25:23.780
It was always triggered for backspin or something like that, that you couldn't apply two different sets of controls for, for the same, uh, recognition.

00:25:23.780 --> 00:25:24.720
Maybe. Yeah.

00:25:25.100 --> 00:25:29.780
And then the last notable addition that I wanted to talk about is a practice mode.

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:35.820
You know, this was something that we all had a gripe about in NES golf because there was no way to practice the later holes.

00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:39.500
You just had to remember them and be like, oh yeah, I'm okay at this one.

00:25:39.660 --> 00:25:43.620
If you assume that you could get all the way to the 17th hole again in NES golf.

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:47.160
So in this one, you're able to practice a particular hole that's bugging you.

00:25:47.300 --> 00:25:51.700
And you can also practice better with all the characters too, to better understand how they work.

00:25:51.700 --> 00:25:54.380
And so I think this just makes so much sense.

00:25:54.440 --> 00:26:00.700
And it feels weird that we praising a practice mode here, but we don't have a lot of games that let you practice much of anything.

00:26:01.620 --> 00:26:13.200
Yeah. And I think it was needed a lot more here because like you said, there are these four different characters that you want to be able to play around with and see who you're good with and who you, you know, whose control schemes you like.

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:24.500
One thing I wanted to mention was in the, I guess the campaign mode, if you, for lack of a better word, I guess.

00:26:24.500 --> 00:26:45.260
Um, I liked the additions of holes that had little, uh, mini objectives on them, like the farthest drive and closest in, um, and they would, you would get like, you'd get a trophy for that even while you're just going through a normal nine holes.

00:26:45.540 --> 00:26:50.160
Uh, so I guess little tidbits like that were also a good improvement.

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:52.180
I thought added some flavor to it.

00:26:53.140 --> 00:26:55.560
Yeah. I mean, there's gotta be something there.

00:26:55.700 --> 00:27:07.040
I don't watch golf if it's not clear there, but there's gotta be something there because that's even a mechanic in Mario golf on the N64 where certain courses, certain holes would just be like, who's got the longest drive.

00:27:07.220 --> 00:27:08.800
And then you would get a trophy for that too.

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:10.480
So that must be like a thing in golf.

00:27:10.900 --> 00:27:11.980
I don't actually know.

00:27:12.220 --> 00:27:14.020
I liked, I liked that inclusion.

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:17.440
Is that what, what Nassau is?

00:27:17.560 --> 00:27:18.840
That's the Nassau. Yes.

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:21.280
I got it. It's like the Nassau classic or something.

00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:22.540
I don't know. Yeah.

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:24.180
I mean, isn't that what that is?

00:27:24.260 --> 00:27:29.520
I know that's like betting on three different things, but is that like actually betting on specific?

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:31.340
Isn't that what that is?

00:27:31.420 --> 00:27:32.660
Isn't that like. Yeah.

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:34.500
It's a wager. It's a wager in golf.

00:27:34.720 --> 00:27:36.860
Yeah. Oh, okay.

00:27:36.980 --> 00:27:37.940
I didn't know that.

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:46.780
Yeah. I don't really know too much more about it, but I thought, I think it's like you bet on like the first half of the game and you bet on the first nine holes in the back nine and then like something else.

00:27:46.780 --> 00:27:47.960
Interesting. Yeah.

00:27:48.020 --> 00:27:53.700
I just took it as here's, you're going to go and play the whole course versus a computer or somebody else.

00:27:53.820 --> 00:27:57.560
I didn't know. It's money, but in this game, obviously there's no money.

00:27:57.560 --> 00:28:03.800
So it's just wagered on the best score of each of the front nine back nine for the total of 18 holes.

00:28:03.800 --> 00:28:18.960
So it's the same thing as like what I think NES golf called match play, where you just basically get you get appropriate points based on a particular hole, not in terms of strokes.

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:28.880
So there's no difference if one guy gets like negative seven and the other guy, you know, has plus three because it only counts like who won the most amount of holes.

00:28:29.120 --> 00:28:31.000
It's an individual type thing.

00:28:31.140 --> 00:28:36.080
And I think that's really cool. I think that's a neat way to get around being bad at particular holes.

00:28:36.860 --> 00:28:38.420
Yeah, that makes sense.

00:28:39.340 --> 00:28:46.080
One thing I wanted to talk about was I appreciated the improved graphics in this game.

00:28:46.820 --> 00:29:13.300
Not that they are anything like special, but instead of just the bare bones, like grass and sand and water of NES golf, like there's actually some almost 3D elements in one of the window panes in this where like the tree, like you see trees in the distance and the trees that you actually interact with on the course.

00:29:13.880 --> 00:29:22.200
Because the character models are a bit more detailed and they're not just weirdly realistically shaped Mario's.

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:28.080
So I thought that was also something that you could applaud.

00:29:28.400 --> 00:29:32.980
Yeah, I thought I thought it definitely looked like, you know, there was like some shading or so, whatever you want to call it.

00:29:33.080 --> 00:29:34.200
It wasn't just solid colors.

00:29:35.220 --> 00:29:42.300
But there was one thing about actually like the improved look that I actually think hurt it a little bit unless I misinterpreted it.

00:29:42.300 --> 00:29:51.280
But in NES golf, when you're on the green, there were like just little arrows that I kind of look like they're supposed to be part of the aesthetic.

00:29:51.440 --> 00:29:57.100
But they also serve to point as to like which way the slope is like which way your ball will roll.

00:29:57.280 --> 00:29:58.680
Well, that still exists on the green.

00:29:58.720 --> 00:30:02.500
It looks like, yeah, there's something there, but it's like this.

00:30:02.540 --> 00:30:03.760
It's like not always air.

00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:04.780
They're like weird designs.

00:30:04.920 --> 00:30:06.960
I can't even tell sometimes which way it's trying to point.

00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:18.460
Oh, I guess I had a more I took to it a bit more naturally because they do sort of they end up looking like arrows in some instances.

00:30:18.740 --> 00:30:20.660
And sometimes they're in some instances.

00:30:20.960 --> 00:30:27.000
Yeah. But then other instances, they're just not like and sometimes I'm like, OK, like this one line is like out further than the two next to it.

00:30:27.100 --> 00:30:28.300
So it looks like it's pointing that way.

00:30:28.300 --> 00:30:31.800
But then another instances, I'm just like, what is this shape?

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:33.920
So that's what I just kind of guessed sometimes.

00:30:34.340 --> 00:30:35.180
OK. Yeah.

00:30:36.620 --> 00:30:48.780
I think I think what Joe's getting at there, which, you know, it's like a weird fault of the game here where the NES one is being so straightforward about where the direction on the putt goes.

00:30:48.780 --> 00:30:51.320
It's that it's just it's just right angles.

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:53.900
You know, it's just here's here's it's heading left.

00:30:54.080 --> 00:30:59.200
It's heading right. This one does more design work, which which to Sean's point is true.

00:30:59.320 --> 00:31:02.340
You'll be able to tell, but it's not inherently obvious.

00:31:02.620 --> 00:31:06.260
Yeah. And but it's also that's the setting that they were going for.

00:31:06.540 --> 00:31:11.380
Yeah. It's a bit more realistic, even though no green has these weird patterns on them.

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:16.000
But it's also telling you how steep the curve is.

00:31:16.140 --> 00:31:20.260
And there's like eight directions it can go now instead of just four.

00:31:20.660 --> 00:31:25.480
So I think with the with the information they were trying to get across.

00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:30.100
Yeah, it wasn't perfectly clear, but I think it's something you can learn over time.

00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:32.480
Yeah. I mean, I'm sure.

00:31:32.600 --> 00:31:34.740
Yeah, I'm sure the more you play it, the more you can pick up.

00:31:34.820 --> 00:31:37.720
I don't remember NES golf only having four directions.

00:31:37.900 --> 00:31:38.800
Is that are we sure?

00:31:39.180 --> 00:31:40.220
Maybe that was false.

00:31:40.220 --> 00:31:43.880
But yeah, I didn't I also didn't realize that this one had steeper.

00:31:44.280 --> 00:31:45.960
That's cool that it does have steeper things.

00:31:46.120 --> 00:31:49.160
But the thing that bothers me is I almost think it feels a little less realistic.

00:31:49.340 --> 00:31:52.120
Because I feel like if I'm on a golf course, even though there are no arrows indicating,

00:31:52.220 --> 00:31:55.500
it's very clear to me where the slope is, like, especially when you're on there.

00:31:55.880 --> 00:31:57.980
Whereas like here, I don't know, maybe I have to play it again.

00:31:58.060 --> 00:32:04.660
Maybe I missed something. But here it felt like I was like, I was like never 100% sure that I was right about which

00:32:04.660 --> 00:32:07.140
way the slope was going and how, you know, how steep it was.

00:32:08.320 --> 00:32:14.320
The manual rightly assumes that most kids won't understand all of the rules of golf.

00:32:14.560 --> 00:32:19.840
And I think the manual does a great job here covering a lot of weird instances that might

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:21.480
seem like, oh, that's kind of unfair.

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:23.860
But then you read the manual and say, oh, well, they did mention it.

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:25.340
So I guess I don't have an excuse.

00:32:25.340 --> 00:32:30.780
And that's one of those things where it does say that, you know, specifically on the putting

00:32:30.780 --> 00:32:34.680
green, the ball goes in the direction of the point of the mark.

00:32:34.840 --> 00:32:36.980
The bigger the mark, the more the ball is affected.

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:42.140
And then even goes as far as to say that the influence of grass on the American course is

00:32:42.140 --> 00:32:44.180
stronger than on the Japanese course.

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:46.360
Interesting. Damn right.

00:32:47.060 --> 00:32:48.960
We have stronger grass over here.

00:32:49.080 --> 00:32:52.620
American grass. Yeah, I didn't read any of that.

00:32:52.720 --> 00:32:55.580
So that might also be where my issue comes in.

00:32:56.960 --> 00:32:58.960
To be continued.

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:02.520
To be continued.

00:33:02.520 --> 00:33:11.240
To be continued.

00:33:25.720 --> 00:33:31.300
And since we're talking about the graphics, we might as well talk about the music, because this is our first golf game with music.

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:42.700
Oh, yeah. Well, I'll let you know my first impression of it was it felt very, for some reason it was feeling very Johto region to me.

00:33:42.820 --> 00:33:44.340
It felt very Pokemon Gen 2.

00:33:45.260 --> 00:33:48.100
I don't know if anyone else got that, but that's what it was reminding me of.

00:33:48.200 --> 00:33:51.780
Well, it does have a bit of a, it has a bit of a Game Boy sound to it.

00:33:51.820 --> 00:33:54.240
I'm glad you brought that up because I'm listening to it right now.

00:33:54.240 --> 00:34:02.760
And yeah, it doesn't quite sound like an NES style of music, but it obviously is that they can't change chips or anything.

00:34:03.020 --> 00:34:14.780
It just seems so distinctly, and maybe it's just the number of sound channels they're using, but it just seems so distinctly like upbeat and high use of chimes and everything like that.

00:34:14.840 --> 00:34:16.420
So I think that's really cool that you mentioned that.

00:34:16.560 --> 00:34:23.960
Right. And you mentioned the number of sound channels, and I think that's part of the reason where, because I think part of the reason where I'm making the connection is because,

00:34:24.400 --> 00:34:30.140
the first generation of Pokemon, I felt like it was like much simpler music, and the second one got a little more complex.

00:34:30.480 --> 00:34:34.760
I feel like this is like a little more complex than what we're used to hearing on the NES.

00:34:35.560 --> 00:34:38.020
I think it's giving me like some nostalgia there.

00:34:38.300 --> 00:34:40.100
Name drop. Yeah, some nostalgia.

00:34:40.340 --> 00:34:41.280
I didn't even think of that.

00:34:41.280 --> 00:34:53.120
And I don't want to go like too deep into the differences between like stroke play and Nassau mode as if they're like, you know, as if like, oh, like we have to get into the nitty gritty of these.

00:34:53.120 --> 00:35:06.060
But, you know, with stroke play being solo and Nassau finally giving players the ability to verse computers where NES Golf didn't, I thought it was fun to play against computers.

00:35:06.060 --> 00:35:09.960
But they're pretty, they're pretty, pretty good.

00:35:10.500 --> 00:35:20.580
I think it is. Okay, so I had a situation where I played as Lee Trevino and, I'm sorry, Super Max.

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:24.780
And then it randomly chose Pretty Amy.

00:35:24.780 --> 00:35:29.160
And I wasn't doing too great, but I was holding my own.

00:35:29.180 --> 00:35:38.960
And I wouldn't have won a hole unless the AI for Amy actually, I thought like, was actually trying to rebel against me.

00:35:39.400 --> 00:35:41.520
Because I got pretty lucky.

00:35:41.880 --> 00:35:43.340
I forgot which hole it was.

00:35:43.820 --> 00:35:45.020
I birdied it.

00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:51.840
And so Amy comes up on the green and she overshoots it.

00:35:52.600 --> 00:35:57.040
And now she's in the very rough or the super rough.

00:35:57.120 --> 00:35:58.860
I don't know what kind of rough it was.

00:35:58.980 --> 00:36:01.100
And so she's trying to get out.

00:36:01.400 --> 00:36:04.480
And then she doesn't hit the green again.

00:36:05.320 --> 00:36:07.580
And it's her, it's her stroke again.

00:36:07.580 --> 00:36:12.660
And then she just starts rotating away from the hole further into the rough.

00:36:13.220 --> 00:36:16.400
That exact, that exact thing happened to me, but it wasn't Pretty Amy.

00:36:16.500 --> 00:36:21.820
It's almost as if like the computer assumes that the hole is in the hole.

00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:31.140
It was in a different spot because they, this, the computer did this 17 times before it finally randomly got in the hole.

00:36:31.480 --> 00:36:33.600
Or like 15, something along those lines.

00:36:33.820 --> 00:36:43.520
Before it finally got in the hole by what I assume was black magic because it was off of the, the, the roughest part of the course possible.

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:46.360
And it was just like a straight shot into the hole.

00:36:46.760 --> 00:36:50.300
So I don't know what the, what was going on with the AI in this.

00:36:50.400 --> 00:36:56.920
